# OptiClear-X: Phase III Clinical Trial Results
Published: January 2024
Principal Investigators: Dr. Sarah Chen, Dr. Michael Roberts
Institution: Vision Research Institute

## Drug Overview
OptiClear-X (ocumapril) is a novel small-molecule drug targeting axial elongation in myopic patients. The drug works by inhibiting TGF-β signaling in the scleral tissue, preventing excessive eye growth associated with myopia progression.

## Chemical Properties
- Formula: C23H29N3O4
- Half-life: 14.2 hours
- Bioavailability: 78% when administered as eye drops
- Storage: Stable at 2-8°C for 24 months

## Clinical Trial Design
- Double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled study
- Duration: 24 months
- Participants: 2,842 patients (ages 12-25)
- Treatment protocol: One drop per eye, twice daily
- Primary endpoint: Change in spherical equivalent refraction
- Secondary endpoint: Axial length change

## Results

### Efficacy
- Mean myopia reduction: -1.75D (±0.3D) at 12 months
- Axial length progression reduced by 67% compared to placebo
- Response rate: 82% of patients showed improvement
- Stabilization achieved in 73% of cases after 18 months

### Subgroup Analysis
Children (12-17):
- Efficacy rate: 88%
- Mean improvement: -2.1D
- Prevention rate: 91%

Adults (18-25):
- Efficacy rate: 76%
- Mean improvement: -1.4D
- Prevention rate: 84%

### Safety Profile
Adverse events:
- Mild eye irritation: 12%
- Temporary blurred vision: 8%
- Photophobia: 5%
- Serious adverse events: <0.1%

